    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d54">

                <pb facs="0171" n="71" xml:id="pg71"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.51/1137</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Geddes</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-06-25"><hi rendition="#italic">June 25,
                        1920</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 395</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to your note of March
                        29th<anchor n="13" xml:id="d54-f1"/> I have the honour to inform you that
                    British bondholders of the Liberian Loan are complaining of the non-application
                    of the Sinking Fund and that His Majesty’s Government understand the funds
                    applicable to this purpose are being retained by the American Receiver.</p>
                <p>If this understanding is correct my Government are anxious to make it clear that,
                    in their view, the Liberian Loan including the Sinking Fund, should be met to
                    the limit permitted by the Receivership receipts and without reference to the
                    question of exchange.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">A. C. Geddes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
